WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.900
Every amino acid story has three acts—structure, charge, and behavior.

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.960
This roadmap shows how an amino acid's backbone and R group shape its charge at

3
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.800
physiological pH, and how structure and charge influence what it does—folding,

4
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.100
binding, catalysis.

5
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.080
Keep this sequence in mind—structure first, charge second, function third.

6
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:31.753
At physiological pH, free amino acids have oppositely charged amino and carboxyl

7
00:00:31.753 --> 00:00:35.593
groups. Their charges cancel unless the side chain adds a charge.

8
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:41.253
Drop the pH and the carboxylate gets protonated; raise it and the amino group loses

9
00:00:41.253 --> 00:00:47.053
its proton. The zwitterion dominates between those pKas, and at the isoelectric

10
00:00:47.053 --> 00:00:50.293
point, or pI, the average net charge is zero.

11
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:54.367
Let's pin down the rule you'll use for the rest of biochemistry.

12
00:00:55.227 --> 00:00:57.467
It's a seesaw between pH and pKa.

13
00:00:57.467 --> 00:01:01.247
When the pH is greater than the pKa, the group gives up its proton.

14
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:02.667
It's deprotonated.

15
00:01:03.187 --> 00:01:06.247
When the pH is less than the pKa, the group holds on.

16
00:01:06.727 --> 00:01:08.907
It's protonated. That's the whole rule.

17
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:11.587
pH above pKa, a proton off.

18
00:01:12.187 --> 00:01:14.367
pH below pKa, a proton on.

19
00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:20.267
Drill it until it's automatic, because every titration curve, every buffer, and

20
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:24.527
every charged side chain you meet from here on comes back to this one comparison.

21
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.827
Titration is a three-step experiment.

22
00:01:29.547 --> 00:01:34.427
You add acid or base from a burette, monitor the pH in real time, and plot the

23
00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:38.407
resulting curve. What the curve reveals is remarkably informative.

24
00:01:39.187 --> 00:01:41.067
Each plateau's center gives a pKa.

25
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:47.207
For glycine, shown here, the average of its two pKas gives the isoelectric point,

26
00:01:47.527 --> 00:01:49.407
where the average net charge is zero.

27
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.280
When an amino acid has an ionizable side chain, finding its isoelectric point

28
00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.580
follows a simple shortcut.

29
00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:58.820
First, draw a charge ladder.

30
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.760
Listing all protonation states from most positive at the top to most negative at the

31
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.660
bottom, locate the net-zero zwitterionic form.

32
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.840
The pI is simply the average of the two pKa values that bracket that neutral

33
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:17.560
species. Find the neutral form first, then average the pKa values on either side.

34
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:24.230
Histidine's side-chain pKa is roughly 6.0 in free solution; protein environments can

35
00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:30.250
shift it. At pH 7.4, its side chain is mostly neutral, though some is protonated.

36
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:35.510
This chemistry makes histidine useful in enzyme active sites, metal coordination,

37
00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:41.010
and pH sensing. Think of it as a molecular switch whose balance depends on its

38
00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.030
surroundings, ready to donate or accept a proton.
